{"name":"Frozen Tundra","hex":"#a3bfcb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"frozen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having undergone the process of freezing; in ice form.","Immobilized."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["past participle of freeze"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frozen"},{"term":"tundra","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flat and treeless Arctic biome.","A long stretch of something, such as time."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tundra"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lambeau Field","blocks":[{"text":"Lambeau Field is an outdoor athletic stadium in the north central United States, located in Green Bay, Wisconsin. The home field of the Green Bay Packers of the National Football League (NFL), it opened 69 years ago in 1957 as City Stadium, replacing the original City Stadium at East High School as the Packers' home field. Informally known as New City Stadium for its first eight seasons, it was renamed in August 1965 in memory of Packers founder, player, and long-time head coach, Earl \"Curly\" Lambeau, who had died two months earlier."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Lambeau_Field_-_Green_Bay_Packers_Football_Stadium_-_Wisconsin.jpg/330px-Lambeau_Field_-_Green_Bay_Packers_Football_Stadium_-_Wisconsin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lambeau_Field","wikidata":"Q860790"},{"title":"Tundra","blocks":[{"text":"In physical geography, a tundra is a type of biome where tree growth is hindered by frigid temperatures and short growing seasons. There are three regions and associated types of tundra: Arctic, Alpine, and Antarctic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Greenland_scoresby-sydkapp2_hg.jpg/330px-Greenland_scoresby-sydkapp2_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w